# cesvu CLI

Encrypt/decrypt `.cesvu` v1 files from the command line — a
zero-knowledge, libsodium-backed format compatible with the
[cesvu.com](https://cesvu.com) web app.

See `../../.cesvu SPEC/README.md` for the wire format both implementations share.

## Install (editable, for development)

```
pip install -e .
```

## Usage

```
cesvu encrypt path/to/File.txt
cesvu encrypt path/to/File.txt --diceware
cesvu encrypt path/to/File.txt --emoji
cesvu encrypt path/to/File.txt -p "my custom password"

cesvu decrypt path/to/File.txt.cesvu
cesvu decrypt path/to/File.txt.cesvu -p "my custom password"
```

### Viewer vs. download: `.txt`/`.md`/`.csv` open in the cesvu.com viewer by default

When you encrypt a file whose extension is `.txt`, `.md`, or `.csv`, `cesvu
encrypt` wraps it in the same document framing the web app's own **Export**
button produces. The result opens directly in the [cesvu.com](https://cesvu.com)
viewer when imported there — not just downloaded as a plain file.

Every other file type (`.pdf`, `.docx`, `.xlsx`, images, `.zip`, etc.) is
unaffected by this and always encrypts as a plain file the recipient
downloads, exactly as before — those formats need real parsing/extraction to
populate the viewer's document fields, which isn't implemented in this CLI
(only in the browser itself).

Pass `--download` to opt a `.txt`/`.md`/`.csv` file out of this and force the
old plain-file behavior instead (the recipient always gets a download, never
the in-app viewer):

```
cesvu encrypt notes.txt              # opens in the cesvu.com viewer
cesvu encrypt notes.txt --download   # always downloads, viewer not used
```

`--download` has no effect on file types that already always download
(e.g. `.pdf`, `.zip`) — there's nothing for it to opt out of there.

#### Per-type fidelity: matches what the browser would have produced

Each auto-framed type mirrors exactly what cesvu.com's own upload/export
flow does for the same file, not just its raw bytes:

- **`.txt`** — the file's UTF-8 text (BOM stripped), trimmed of leading/
  trailing whitespace. Unicode (including emoji) is preserved as literal
  characters, not escaped.
- **`.md`** — the markdown is **stripped to plain text** (headings, bold/
  italic, links, code fences, lists, etc. removed) before being stored,
  matching the web app's own markdown parser. The viewer uses this plain
  text for reading/notes, the same as an in-browser upload would.
- **`.csv`** — the raw CSV bytes travel through unmodified (any encoding —
  they are never decoded as UTF-8), so the cesvu.com viewer reconstructs
  the full interactive table exactly as if the CSV had been uploaded
  directly in the browser, instead of showing a blank/empty view.

#### `cesvu decrypt` automatically unwraps this framing back to plain content

Decrypting a `.txt`/`.md`/`.csv` file that was encrypted with this document
framing writes out just the underlying content — the plain text or CSV
rows — not the internal CFP1 envelope (magic bytes + JSON metadata) that
carries it. For example, decrypting an emoji passphrase saved as
`password.txt` produces a file containing only the emoji, exactly as
originally written — never the surrounding
`{"id": ..., "sourceType": "txt", "text": "...", ...}` JSON.

This detection is intentionally conservative: it requires the CFP1 magic
bytes, a length field that fits the actual decrypted data, and JSON that
parses into the expected document shape, all at once, before unwrapping
anything. Any file that doesn't meet every one of those checks — including
a `--download`-forced file, any other file type, or a file that merely
happens to start with the same 4 magic bytes — is written out exactly as
decrypted, unchanged.


### Emoji passphrases: use `--password-file`, not `-p`, on the console



Typing or pasting an emoji passphrase directly into a terminal — especially
Windows' `cmd.exe`/PowerShell consoles — can silently corrupt it (emoji are
outside the Basic Multilingual Plane and get mangled into invalid lone
UTF-16 surrogate characters, which then fail with a `UnicodeEncodeError`
when the tool tries to derive a key from them). To avoid this entirely,
save the passphrase to a UTF-8 text file and pass it with `--password-file`:

```
cesvu encrypt path/to/File.txt --emoji
cesvu decrypt path/to/File.txt.cesvu --password-file emoji-passphrase.txt
```

If a passphrase supplied via `-p` or the interactive prompt is detected to
contain invalid/corrupted characters, `cesvu` fails with a clear error
message pointing you at `--password-file` instead of a raw traceback.

**`cesvu decrypt` automatically detects when a file requires an emoji
passphrase** (`passProfile = Pass_Profile_Emoji` in the file's header) and
adapts its behavior accordingly — **on Windows only** (`sys.platform ==
"win32"`), since this corruption is essentially specific to `cmd.exe`/
PowerShell's default non-UTF-8 console code page. Linux/macOS terminals
are overwhelmingly UTF-8 by default, so `-p` and the normal interactive
prompt are left available there as usual:

- **On Windows**, `-p`/`--password` is disallowed for emoji-profile files
  and fails immediately with a clear error, since there's no safe way to
  pass emoji through that flag from a default Windows console. If no `-p`,
  `--password-file`, or `CESVU_PASSPHRASE` env var is given, `cesvu`
  will **not** fall back to the normal (`getpass`) interactive prompt for
  these files — instead it prints a short notice and asks you to enter the
  **path to a UTF-8 text file** containing the emoji passphrase, then
  reads it the same way `--password-file` does.
- **On Linux/macOS**, `-p` and the normal interactive prompt work as usual
  for emoji-profile files. If a passphrase still comes through corrupted
  anyway (e.g. an unusual `LANG=C`/non-UTF-8 locale), the same
  invalid-character check still catches it and reports a clear error
  pointing you at `--password-file`, instead of an opaque
  `UnicodeEncodeError`.
- `--password-file` and `CESVU_PASSPHRASE` continue to work exactly the
  same on every OS for emoji-profile files.




### Preamble

Every encrypted file carries the same canonical preamble text as the web
app (a short notice shown before the passphrase prompt). There is no
option to customize it — `cesvu encrypt` always writes `DEFAULT_PREAMBLE`
verbatim, and `cesvu decrypt` does not read, display, or otherwise inspect
whatever preamble text is present in a file's header (it's skipped as
opaque bytes; only its declared length matters for locating the rest of
the header).


## License

This CLI is distributed under the **Apache License, Version 2.0**. The
full text ships inside the installed package at
`<site-packages>/cesvu/LICENSE.txt` (a copy of the project's
`LICENSE` file).

The CLI depends on two direct packages — `pynacl` and `tqdm` — plus
their transitive closure (`cffi`, `pycparser`, and so on). The full
text of every upstream license that affects this distribution is
bundled verbatim into the installed package at
`<site-packages>/cesvu/licenses/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt`. A short
top-level notice lives at `<site-packages>/cesvu/NOTICE.txt`.

If you've cloned the repository and want to verify or rebuild the
third-party notice from your local pip environment (for example, after
upgrading a dep), see
[`REGENERATING_THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt`](REGENERATING_THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt).

